// Default FAQ content for the in-app FAQ page (moved out of index.html).
window.DEFAULT_FAQ = {
  title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
  subtitle: 'Technical guidance on heat pump selection, performance, and UK regulations.',
  sections: [
    {id:'f1',title:'What is COP and why does it matter?',short:'COP (Coefficient of Performance) is a heat pump\'s efficiency ratio — heat output divided by electricity input. A COP of 4 means 4 kW of heat for every 1 kW of power, with the rest drawn free from the environment. It always depends on test conditions (e.g. A7/W35), so only compare COP values measured at the same conditions.',body:'<p>COP stands for <strong>Coefficient of Performance</strong> and is the primary measure of a heat pump\'s efficiency. It expresses the ratio of useful heat output to electrical energy input. A heat pump with a COP of 4.0 produces 4 kilowatts of heat for every 1 kilowatt of electricity consumed — the additional 3 kW comes free from the environment (air, ground, or water).</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 280" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="280" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <!-- Input arrow -->  <rect x="40" y="140" width="120" height="60" rx="8" fill="#0F2B2B"/>  <text x="100" y="167" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="11" font-weight="600">ELECTRICITY IN</text>  <text x="100" y="183" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="14" font-weight="700">1 kW</text>  <!-- Arrow -->  <path d="M160 170 L200 170" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>  <defs><marker id="arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="#0D7377"/></marker></defs>  <!-- Heat pump box -->  <rect x="200" y="110" width="140" height="120" rx="10" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="270" y="157" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="12" font-weight="600">HEAT PUMP</text>  <text x="270" y="177" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="11">COP = 4.0</text>  <!-- Free energy arrow -->  <path d="M270 240 L270 230" stroke="#3ECCC0" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>  <defs><marker id="arr2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="#3ECCC0"/></marker></defs>  <text x="270" y="260" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">FREE ENERGY FROM AIR/GROUND/WATER</text>  <text x="270" y="275" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">(3 kW absorbed)</text>  <!-- Output arrow -->  <path d="M340 170 L380 170" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>  <!-- Output box -->  <rect x="380" y="140" width="140" height="60" rx="8" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="450" y="167" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="11" font-weight="600">HEAT OUTPUT</text>  <text x="450" y="183" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="14" font-weight="700">4 kW</text>  <!-- Labels -->  <text x="270" y="30" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">How COP Works: 1 kW electricity in → 4 kW heat out (COP 4.0)</text>  <text x="270" y="50" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="11">The remaining 3 kW is free renewable energy absorbed from the environment</text></svg></div><p>This is fundamentally different from a gas boiler or electric heater. A modern condensing gas boiler achieves around 0.92 COP (92% efficiency) — it can never produce more heat energy than the fuel it burns. A direct electric heater has a COP of exactly 1.0. A heat pump routinely delivers 3 to 5 times more heat energy than the electrical energy it uses, making it by far the most efficient form of heating available.</p><p><strong>Why the test conditions matter.</strong> COP is not a fixed number — it varies with outdoor temperature, the temperature of the water being produced, and the operating load. Manufacturers quote COP at a specific test point defined by European standards (EN 14511). The most common is <strong>A7/W35</strong>, meaning 7°C outdoor air temperature and 35°C flow water temperature. This is a favourable test point: mild weather, low flow temperature. In colder weather or when producing higher temperature water for radiators, the COP will be lower.</p><p>This is why comparing COP values between products only makes sense if they were tested at the same conditions. A unit quoting COP 5.0 at A7/W35 is not necessarily better than one quoting COP 3.5 at A7/W55 — the second unit is being tested at a much harder operating point (55°C flow temperature vs 35°C). In this database, every COP value is displayed alongside its test conditions using colour-coded labels so you can make fair comparisons.</p><p><strong>Practical implications.</strong> For UK homeowners, COP directly affects running costs. With electricity at approximately 24p/kWh and gas at 7p/kWh, a heat pump needs a COP of at least 3.4 to match gas boiler running costs. Most modern heat pumps comfortably exceed this when properly installed with appropriate flow temperatures. With underfloor heating at 35°C flow, seasonal COPs of 4.0 or above are achievable, cutting heating bills substantially compared to gas.</p><p>Higher COP also means lower carbon emissions. The UK electricity grid is increasingly decarbonised, so a heat pump with a COP of 3.5 typically produces around 60–75% less CO₂ than a gas boiler for the same amount of heat delivered.</p>'},
    {id:'f2',title:'What is the difference between COP and SCOP?',short:'COP is efficiency at one fixed test condition; SCOP (Seasonal COP) is the average efficiency across a whole heating season, accounting for varying outdoor temperatures. SCOP is the more realistic figure for estimating real-world running costs and is the basis of the EU energy label and grant eligibility.',body:'<p><strong>COP</strong> (Coefficient of Performance) is measured at a single set of conditions — a snapshot of efficiency at one specific outdoor temperature and one specific flow temperature. <strong>SCOP</strong> (Seasonal Coefficient of Performance) is the average efficiency across an entire heating season, accounting for the full range of outdoor temperatures the unit will experience.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 280" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="280" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">COP vs SCOP: Point Test vs Seasonal Average</text>  <!-- Axes -->  <line x1="70" y1="240" x2="520" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <line x1="70" y1="50" x2="70" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <!-- Y axis labels -->  <text x="60" y="244" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">1.0</text>  <text x="60" y="196" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">2.0</text>  <text x="60" y="149" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">3.0</text>  <text x="60" y="101" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">4.0</text>  <text x="60" y="54" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">5.0</text>  <text x="30" y="150" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10" transform="rotate(-90,30,150)">COP</text>  <!-- X axis labels -->  <text x="120" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">-15°C</text>  <text x="210" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">-7°C</text>  <text x="300" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">2°C</text>  <text x="390" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">7°C</text>  <text x="480" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">15°C</text>  <text x="300" y="275" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Outdoor Air Temperature</text>  <!-- COP curve -->  <path d="M120 210 C180 195 240 160 300 130 C360 100 420 80 480 60" fill="none" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="2.5"/>  <!-- SCOP average line -->  <line x1="70" y1="130" x2="520" y2="130" stroke="#3ECCC0" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="8,4"/>  <!-- COP point marker at A7 -->  <circle cx="390" cy="91" r="6" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="320" y="92" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="10" font-weight="600">COP 4.0 at A7</text>  <!-- SCOP label -->  <text x="455" y="126" text-anchor="start" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="10" font-weight="600">SCOP 3.5</text>  <!-- Legend -->  <line x1="100" y1="45" x2="120" y2="45" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="2.5"/>  <text x="125" y="49" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="10">COP at each temperature</text>  <line x1="280" y1="45" x2="300" y2="45" stroke="#3ECCC0" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="8,4"/>  <text x="305" y="49" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="10">SCOP (seasonal average)</text></svg></div><p>Think of COP as a car\'s fuel economy at a constant 60 mph on a flat motorway — useful but not representative. SCOP is the average fuel economy over a year of real driving including city traffic, hills, and cold starts. SCOP gives a much more realistic picture of what your energy bills will actually be.</p><p><strong>How SCOP is calculated.</strong> The European standard EN 14825 defines SCOP testing. The heat pump\'s COP is measured at four outdoor temperature points: −7°C, 2°C, 7°C, and 12°C (for the "average" European climate zone that the UK falls into). These are then weighted by how many hours per year each temperature range occurs in a typical heating season, producing a single seasonal figure.</p><p>For example, a heat pump might achieve COP 5.0 at 12°C, COP 4.2 at 7°C, COP 3.0 at 2°C, and COP 2.2 at −7°C. The weighted average across a UK heating season might yield an SCOP of 3.8. This single number is far more useful for estimating running costs than any individual COP figure.</p><p><strong>Why SCOP matters for UK grants and regulations.</strong> The UK\'s Boiler Upgrade Scheme and MCS certification both reference SCOP. To qualify for the £7,500 BUS grant, a heat pump must achieve a minimum SCOP (Space Heating Seasonal Coefficient of Performance) of 2.8 when measured under EN 14825. Most modern units comfortably exceed this — typical residential ASHPs achieve SCOP values between 3.5 and 5.0.</p><p>The EU Energy Label also uses SCOP to assign efficiency ratings. An SCOP of 3.6 or above at 35°C flow typically earns an A++ rating, while 4.6 or above earns A+++. When browsing this database, SCOP is shown where manufacturers provide it, and it is generally the more reliable figure for estimating real-world performance and comparing products.</p><p><strong>The gap between COP and SCOP.</strong> SCOP is almost always lower than the headline COP figure because it includes colder weather operation where efficiency drops. A typical residential ASHP might quote COP 4.5 at A7/W35 but achieve SCOP 3.8. The gap between COP and SCOP tells you how sensitive the unit is to cold weather — a smaller gap indicates the unit maintains its performance better as temperatures drop. This is particularly important in colder UK regions like Scotland or northern England.</p>'},
    {id:'f3',title:'ASHP vs GSHP vs WSHP — which is right for my project?',short:'ASHP (air source) draws heat from outdoor air — cheapest and most common, ~90% of UK installs. GSHP (ground source) uses buried pipes — higher efficiency and steadier output, but costlier to install. WSHP (water source) uses a river, lake, or aquifer — very efficient where a suitable water body exists. The right choice depends on your site, budget, and space.',body:'<p>The three types of heat pump differ in where they extract their renewable energy from. Each has distinct advantages, costs, and site requirements. Choosing the right type depends on your property, budget, and what\'s physically possible on your site.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 280" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="280" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Heat Pump Types: Source Comparison</text>  <!-- ASHP -->  <rect x="20" y="55" width="160" height="200" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <text x="100" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="14" font-weight="700">ASHP</text>  <text x="100" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Air Source</text>  <text x="100" y="120" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Source: Outdoor air</text>  <text x="100" y="138" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">COP: 3.5 – 5.0</text>  <text x="100" y="156" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Install: Easiest</text>  <text x="100" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Cost: £8k – £15k</text>  <text x="100" y="198" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ Most common</text>  <text x="100" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ No ground works</text>  <text x="100" y="230" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ Lower COP in cold</text>  <text x="100" y="246" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ Outdoor noise</text>  <!-- GSHP -->  <rect x="200" y="55" width="160" height="200" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#6558A6" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <text x="280" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6558A6" font-size="14" font-weight="700">GSHP</text>  <text x="280" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Ground Source</text>  <text x="280" y="120" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Source: Ground loops</text>  <text x="280" y="138" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">COP: 4.0 – 5.5</text>  <text x="280" y="156" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Install: Complex</text>  <text x="280" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Cost: £18k – £35k</text>  <text x="280" y="198" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6558A6" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ Highest COP</text>  <text x="280" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6558A6" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ No outdoor unit</text>  <text x="280" y="230" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ Needs land/drilling</text>  <text x="280" y="246" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ Higher upfront cost</text>  <!-- WSHP -->  <rect x="380" y="55" width="160" height="200" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#A66D1E" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <text x="460" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="14" font-weight="700">WSHP</text>  <text x="460" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Water Source</text>  <text x="460" y="120" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Source: River/lake/bore</text>  <text x="460" y="138" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">COP: 4.5 – 6.0</text>  <text x="460" y="156" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Install: Specialist</text>  <text x="460" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">Cost: £20k – £40k+</text>  <text x="460" y="198" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ Best COP possible</text>  <text x="460" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="10" font-weight="500">✓ Stable source temp</text>  <text x="460" y="230" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ Needs water access</text>  <text x="460" y="246" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">✗ EA permits needed</text></svg></div><p><strong>Air Source Heat Pumps (ASHP)</strong> are by far the most common choice in the UK, accounting for over 90% of residential installations. They extract heat from outdoor air using a fan and refrigerant cycle. The outdoor unit is roughly the size of a large air conditioning condenser and is typically installed on a concrete plinth beside the property. Installation is straightforward — no ground works, no boreholes, no water source permissions. A typical residential ASHP installation costs £8,000–£15,000 including the hot water cylinder and controls.</p><p>The trade-off is that ASHPs are affected by outdoor air temperature. As the air gets colder, the COP drops and the unit has to work harder. Modern inverter-driven ASHPs handle this well — many maintain rated capacity down to −20°C or below — but SCOP will always be lower than an equivalent ground or water source unit. ASHPs also produce outdoor noise, which must comply with permitted development limits (42 dB(A) at the nearest neighbour\'s window under MCS 020).</p><p><strong>Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHP)</strong> extract heat from the ground via buried pipes (horizontal trenches or vertical boreholes). Ground temperature in the UK is a stable 8–12°C year-round at depths below 1.5 metres, providing a much more consistent heat source than outdoor air. This stability means GSHPs deliver higher and more consistent COPs — typically 4.0 to 5.5 at B0/W35. The indoor unit is quiet and compact since there is no outdoor fan.</p><p>The downside is cost and disruption. Horizontal ground loops require significant garden area (roughly 2–3 times the floor area of the property) and the trenching is disruptive. Vertical boreholes are less land-intensive but require specialist drilling rigs and geological surveys, with borehole costs alone running £8,000–£15,000 per borehole. Total installed cost for a GSHP system is typically £18,000–£35,000. GSHPs make most sense for new builds (where ground works are already happening), rural properties with large gardens, or sites where noise is a critical constraint.</p><p><strong>Water Source Heat Pumps (WSHP)</strong> extract heat from a body of water — a river, lake, open-loop borehole, or even a mine water source. Water provides the best and most stable heat source of all three types, with entering water temperatures typically 8–15°C. WSHPs routinely achieve COPs of 4.5 to 6.0, the highest of any heat pump type.</p><p>However, WSHPs require access to a suitable water source, which limits their applicability. Open-loop systems abstracting from rivers or aquifers require an Environment Agency abstraction licence. Closed-loop lake or river systems need suitable water body proximity. Installation requires specialist hydraulic design and water quality assessment. WSHPs are most commonly used in larger commercial and district heating applications where the higher efficiency justifies the additional complexity and permitting.</p><p>For most UK homeowners, an ASHP is the practical choice. The technology is mature, installer availability is good, costs are manageable (especially with the £7,500 BUS grant), and modern units perform well even in Scottish winters. GSHPs and WSHPs should be considered where site conditions are favourable and the higher upfront investment is acceptable.</p>'},
    {id:'f4',title:'R290 vs R32 — which refrigerant is better?',short:'R290 (propane) has a near-zero GWP of 3, excellent efficiency, and supports high flow temperatures, but is flammable so the outdoor unit needs careful siting. R32 has a GWP of 675, is mildly flammable, and is well-established with wide installer familiarity. R290 is the more future-proof choice environmentally; R32 remains very common.',body:'<p>The choice of refrigerant is one of the most important technical decisions in heat pump design, affecting efficiency, environmental impact, safety, and long-term serviceability. The two dominant refrigerants in the current UK residential market are <strong>R32</strong> (a synthetic HFC) and <strong>R290</strong> (propane, a natural hydrocarbon). Both are significant improvements over older options, but they have very different characteristics.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="300" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="25" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Global Warming Potential (GWP) Comparison</text>  <text x="280" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Lower GWP = less environmental impact. Values show CO₂-equivalent per kg of refrigerant.</text>  <!-- Y axis -->  <line x1="90" y1="60" x2="90" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1"/>  <text x="40" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10" font-weight="500" transform="rotate(-90,40,155)">GWP Value</text>  <!-- Y gridlines and labels -->  <line x1="90" y1="240" x2="510" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5"/>  <text x="82" y="244" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">0</text>  <line x1="90" y1="197" x2="510" y2="197" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="201" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">500</text>  <line x1="90" y1="154" x2="510" y2="154" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="158" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">1,000</text>  <line x1="90" y1="111" x2="510" y2="111" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="115" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">1,500</text>  <line x1="90" y1="68" x2="510" y2="68" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="72" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">2,000</text>  <!-- Bars (growing upward from baseline at y=240, scale: 2088 max maps to y=65) -->  <!-- R290: GWP 3 -->  <rect x="110" y="239.5" width="40" height="0.5" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="130" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">3</text>  <text x="130" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R290</text>  <text x="130" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">Propane</text>  <!-- R32: GWP 675 -->  <rect x="180" y="184" width="40" height="56" rx="2" fill="#3ECCC0"/>  <text x="200" y="178" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">675</text>  <text x="200" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R32</text>  <text x="200" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC</text>  <!-- R134a: GWP 1430 -->  <rect x="250" y="121" width="40" height="119" rx="2" fill="#C07B1A"/>  <text x="270" y="115" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C07B1A" font-size="12" font-weight="700">1,430</text>  <text x="270" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R134a</text>  <text x="270" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC</text>  <!-- R410A: GWP 2088 -->  <rect x="320" y="67" width="40" height="173" rx="2" fill="#B03030"/>  <text x="340" y="61" text-anchor="middle" fill="#B03030" font-size="12" font-weight="700">2,088</text>  <text x="340" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R410A</text>  <text x="340" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC blend</text>  <!-- R744: GWP 1 -->  <rect x="390" y="239.9" width="40" height="0.1" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="410" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">1</text>  <text x="410" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R744</text>  <text x="410" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">CO₂</text>  <!-- R717: GWP 0 -->  <rect x="460" y="240" width="40" height="0" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="480" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">0</text>  <text x="480" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R717</text>  <text x="480" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">Ammonia</text>  <!-- Legend -->  <rect x="110" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="124" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">Natural / ultra-low GWP</text>  <rect x="280" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#3ECCC0"/>  <text x="294" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">Lower HFC</text>  <rect x="380" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#B03030"/>  <text x="394" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">High GWP (phasing down)</text></svg></div><p><strong>R290 (Propane)</strong> is a natural refrigerant with a Global Warming Potential (GWP) of just 3 — essentially negligible. It has excellent thermodynamic properties, delivering high COPs particularly at low ambient temperatures. R290 is the refrigerant of choice for manufacturers positioning themselves for long-term regulatory compliance, as it is completely unaffected by F-Gas phase-down regulations. Vaillant, Viessmann, Ideal Heating, Baxi, and CTC have all moved their residential ranges to R290.</p><p>The primary consideration with R290 is its flammability — it is classified as A3 (highly flammable) under ISO 817. This limits the refrigerant charge allowed in a single circuit, which in turn limits the capacity of individual units. Most R290 residential ASHPs are monobloc designs where all the refrigerant stays in the outdoor unit, minimising indoor flammability risk. The charge limits mean very large capacities (above about 20 kW) require multiple compressor circuits or cascade arrangements. Installers must hold specific F-Gas qualifications for handling flammable refrigerants.</p><p><strong>R32</strong> is a synthetic HFC with a GWP of 675 — substantially lower than the R410A (GWP 2088) it largely replaced, but still significant from a regulatory perspective. R32 has good efficiency, particularly at higher flow temperatures, and allows larger charge sizes than R290 due to its lower flammability classification (A2L — mildly flammable). This makes it the practical choice for larger capacity split systems and commercial units. Daikin, Samsung, Panasonic, Mitsubishi Electric, Hitachi, LG, and Toshiba all offer R32 units.</p><p>R32\'s main vulnerability is regulatory. The UK F-Gas regulations are phasing down HFC supply based on CO₂-equivalent quotas. As quotas tighten, R32 supply could become more expensive or restricted, potentially affecting servicing costs and refrigerant availability for older installations. This is not an immediate concern — R32 will remain available for servicing existing equipment — but it does mean the industry is trending toward natural refrigerants for new designs.</p><p><strong>Which should you choose?</strong> For a new residential installation, both refrigerants will serve you well. R290 units tend to have marginally higher COPs and complete regulatory certainty. R32 units are available in a wider range of configurations (including split systems) and from more manufacturers. If your priority is maximum long-term future-proofing, R290 has the edge. If you need a specific feature set (cooling capability, split configuration, or very high capacity from a single unit), R32 may be the better fit. In this database, you can filter by refrigerant type to compare products using each.</p>'},
    {id:'f5',title:'What are the UK noise regulations for heat pumps?',short:'In England, an ASHP under permitted development must keep noise at or below 42 dB(A) at the nearest neighbour\'s window (assessed via MCS 020), among other conditions. Exceeding limits can mean planning refusal or relocation. Choose a quiet unit, site it away from boundaries, and check the noise figure and its measurement reference.',body:'<p>Noise is one of the most important practical considerations when installing a heat pump, particularly in densely built residential areas. UK regulations set clear limits, and non-compliance can result in planning refusal, enforcement action, or the need to relocate the unit after installation.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 250" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="250" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Sound Pressure Level vs Distance from Heat Pump</text>  <!-- Heat pump icon -->  <rect x="40" y="90" width="50" height="60" rx="6" fill="#0F2B2B"/>  <text x="65" y="116" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="8" font-weight="600">HP</text>  <text x="65" y="130" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3ECCC0" font-size="8">60 LwA</text>  <!-- Distance markers and dB levels -->  <line x1="90" y1="120" x2="530" y2="120" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4,4"/>  <!-- 1m -->  <circle cx="130" cy="120" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#B03030" stroke-width="2"/>  <text x="130" y="124" text-anchor="middle" fill="#B03030" font-size="11" font-weight="700">52</text>  <text x="130" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">1m</text>  <text x="130" y="168" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">dB(A)</text>  <!-- 3m -->  <circle cx="220" cy="120" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#A66D1E" stroke-width="2"/>  <text x="220" y="124" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="11" font-weight="700">42</text>  <text x="220" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">3m</text>  <text x="220" y="168" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">dB(A)</text>  <!-- 5m -->  <circle cx="310" cy="120" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#3ECCC0" stroke-width="2"/>  <text x="310" y="124" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="11" font-weight="700">38</text>  <text x="310" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">5m</text>  <!-- 10m -->  <circle cx="400" cy="120" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="2"/>  <text x="400" y="124" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="11" font-weight="700">32</text>  <text x="400" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="10">10m</text>  <!-- Context -->  <rect x="40" y="195" width="490" height="40" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#d9e2e2"/>  <text x="65" y="213" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">Reference: 30 dB = quiet bedroom</text>  <text x="65" y="226" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">UK planning limit: 42 dB(A) at nearest neighbour window (MCS 020)</text>  <!-- Neighbour window -->  <rect x="480" y="85" width="30" height="40" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="#3D5C5C" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <line x1="495" y1="85" x2="495" y2="125" stroke="#3D5C5C" stroke-width="1"/>  <line x1="480" y1="105" x2="510" y2="105" stroke="#3D5C5C" stroke-width="1"/>  <text x="495" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="8">WINDOW</text></svg></div><p><strong>Permitted development noise limits.</strong> In England, an air source heat pump can be installed under permitted development rights (without a full planning application) provided it meets several conditions, one of which is noise. The unit must not exceed <strong>42 dB(A)</strong> measured at 1 metre from any neighbouring residential property\'s nearest window or door. This is a sound pressure level (LpA) measurement, not sound power (LwA).</p><p>Understanding the difference is critical. Manufacturers typically quote <strong>sound power level (LwA)</strong> in their datasheets — this is the total acoustic energy output of the unit, measured per EN 12102. Sound power does not change with distance. <strong>Sound pressure level (LpA)</strong> is what you actually hear at a specific distance — it decreases as you move further away. As a rough guide, sound pressure at 1 metre is approximately 6–8 dB lower than the sound power level, and it drops by about 6 dB each time you double the distance.</p><p>So a heat pump with a sound power level of 60 dB(A) LwA would produce approximately 52–54 dB(A) at 1 metre, 46–48 dB(A) at 2 metres, and about 40–42 dB(A) at 4 metres. This means placement is crucial — moving the unit even 1–2 metres further from the neighbour\'s window can make the difference between compliance and refusal.</p><p><strong>MCS 020 planning standard.</strong> The Microgeneration Certification Scheme (MCS) standard MCS 020 provides a methodology for assessing heat pump noise for planning purposes. It requires the installer to calculate the predicted sound pressure level at the nearest noise-sensitive receptor, accounting for distance, any barriers (walls, fences), and reflections from nearby surfaces. The assessment must demonstrate compliance with the 42 dB(A) limit. This assessment is a mandatory part of any MCS-certified installation.</p><p><strong>Practical mitigation measures.</strong> If your preferred installation location is too close to a neighbour, several measures can help. Acoustic barriers (a solid fence or wall between the unit and the receptor) can reduce noise by 5–10 dB. Anti-vibration mounts prevent structure-borne noise transfer. Some manufacturers offer low-noise or quiet modes that reduce fan speed at the expense of slightly lower capacity. Orientation matters too — positioning the fan discharge away from neighbours helps. The NoiseTools.net website (linked on our Useful Links page) provides free calculators for estimating noise propagation and barrier effectiveness.</p><p><strong>Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland</strong> have their own permitted development rules which may differ from England. In Scotland, for example, the noise limit and conditions are broadly similar but the details vary. Always check with your local planning authority if you are unsure whether your installation qualifies for permitted development.</p><p>In this database, noise levels are shown with their measurement reference — either LwA (sound power) or LpA at a stated distance — so you can compare products accurately and assess compliance for your specific installation.</p><div style="margin-top:1rem;padding:14px 18px;background:#E0F5F4;border:1px solid #A3DDD9;border-radius:10px"><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;color:#064E50"><strong>Estimate your noise levels online:</strong> Use the free calculators at <a href="https://noisetools.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#0D7377;font-weight:600">NoiseTools.net</a> to estimate sound pressure levels at distance, model the effect of acoustic barriers, and check whether a heat pump installation is likely to meet the 42 dB planning limit at your neighbour\'s property. It is a useful starting point before commissioning a formal acoustic assessment.</p></div>'},
    {id:'f6',title:'How do I size a heat pump for my property?',short:'Size from a room-by-room heat loss calculation (ideally MCS-compliant), never a rule of thumb. Heat pumps must be sized accurately — oversizing causes short-cycling and lost efficiency, undersizing leaves you cold in winter. Size to the design heat loss at your local design outdoor temperature, accounting for hot water demand.',body:'<p>Correct sizing is arguably the single most important factor in a successful heat pump installation. An oversized unit will short-cycle (turning on and off frequently), reducing efficiency and increasing wear. An undersized unit will struggle to maintain comfort in cold weather. Unlike gas boilers, which are routinely oversized with minimal penalty, heat pumps must be sized accurately to perform well.</p><p><strong>Heat loss calculation.</strong> The starting point is always a room-by-room heat loss calculation, ideally performed to <strong>BS EN 12831</strong> (or the simplified method in MIS 3005 for MCS certification). This calculates how much heat the building loses through its walls, roof, floor, windows, doors, and ventilation at the design outdoor temperature (typically −3°C to −5°C for most of England, colder for Scotland).</p><p>The calculation considers the U-values of every building element (how well insulated each surface is), the total area of each element, the internal-to-external temperature difference, and the ventilation rate. The result is the <strong>design heat loss in kilowatts (kW)</strong> — this is the maximum heating capacity the heat pump needs to deliver on the coldest expected day.</p><p><strong>Typical UK residential requirements:</strong></p><p>A well-insulated modern house (built to current building regulations) might need 3–6 kW. A reasonably insulated 1960s–1990s house typically needs 6–10 kW. A poorly insulated older property with solid walls and single glazing might need 12–20 kW or more. These are rough guides — the actual figure depends entirely on the specific building, its dimensions, construction, and location.</p><p><strong>Why oversizing is harmful.</strong> A gas boiler oversized by 50% simply modulates down or cycles with little efficiency penalty. A heat pump oversized by 50% faces a different problem: it reaches the target temperature too quickly, shuts off, the temperature drops, and it restarts. This short-cycling prevents the compressor from reaching its optimal operating point, reduces the average COP by 10–20%, increases electrical demand from repeated start-up surges, and accelerates compressor wear. Modern inverter-driven heat pumps can modulate down to around 20–30% of rated capacity, which helps, but significant oversizing still causes problems.</p><p><strong>The role of domestic hot water (DHW).</strong> If the heat pump also provides domestic hot water (which it usually does), the hot water demand should be assessed separately. A typical UK household needs 150–250 litres of hot water per day. The heat pump must be capable of reheating the cylinder within a reasonable time, which may require a higher capacity than space heating alone. This is why many residential installations specify 8–12 kW units even for well-insulated homes.</p><p><strong>Buffer tanks and thermal stores.</strong> A buffer tank (typically 50–200 litres) helps manage the mismatch between heat pump capacity and instantaneous demand. It provides thermal mass that smooths out load variations, reduces cycling, and allows the heat pump to run for longer periods at optimal efficiency. Many manufacturers recommend or require a buffer tank, particularly for systems with small radiator circuits.</p><p><strong>The MCS requirement.</strong> For a heat pump installation to qualify for the Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant, it must be designed and installed by an MCS-certified installer who performs the heat loss calculation and sizes the system according to MIS 3005. This standard requires documented evidence of the sizing methodology, ensuring the system is neither significantly over- nor under-sized.</p><p>When using this database, pay attention to both the minimum and maximum capacity figures. A unit listed as 2–10 kW can modulate down to 2 kW in mild weather and ramp up to 10 kW on the coldest days. Ideally, your design heat loss should fall within the upper portion of a unit\'s modulation range — around 60–80% of maximum capacity — leaving headroom for extreme weather without forcing the unit to run at maximum output continuously.</p>'},
    {id:'f7',title:'What electrical supply does a heat pump need?',short:'Most UK homes have single-phase 230V, which suits the majority of residential heat pumps. Larger units (typically above ~12–17 kW) may need three-phase. Always check the peak electrical input (kW) and maximum current draw (amps) against your supply before specifying — an upgrade can be costly and slow.',body:'<p>Every heat pump runs on electricity, and understanding the electrical requirements before installation is essential. The wrong supply can mean costly upgrades, delays, or even prevent installation entirely. The key specifications to check are the <strong>peak electrical input (kW)</strong>, the <strong>supply type</strong> (single-phase or three-phase), and the <strong>maximum current draw (amps)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Single-phase vs three-phase.</strong> Most UK homes have a single-phase electrical supply — a single live wire providing 230V AC. This is sufficient for the majority of residential heat pumps. Typical residential ASHPs draw 1–6 kW of peak electrical input, well within single-phase capability. However, there is an important threshold: installations that can export more than <strong>3.68 kW</strong> (16A per phase) to the grid require notification to your Distribution Network Operator (DNO) under G98/G99 regulations. While a heat pump does not export to the grid, many DNOs apply the same notification threshold to large fixed electrical loads.</p><p>For commercial and larger residential units drawing more than about 6–8 kW peak electrical input, a three-phase supply (400V, three live wires) is usually required. Three-phase provides three times the capacity of single-phase and distributes the load more evenly. If your property does not have three-phase and needs it, the DNO can install one — but this involves significant lead time (typically 8–16 weeks) and cost (£1,000–£5,000+ depending on the distance from the nearest three-phase supply).</p><p><strong>Fuse board and main fuse considerations.</strong> The main fuse (also called the cut-out fuse) at your property determines the maximum current available from the grid. Most UK homes have a 60A or 100A single-phase supply. A heat pump drawing 4 kW at 230V uses approximately 17A. You need to verify that the total electrical demand of the property (including the heat pump) does not exceed the main fuse rating. If it does, the DNO can upgrade the main fuse — usually free of charge but requiring an application.</p><p>The consumer unit (fuse board) may also need upgrading. The heat pump requires a dedicated circuit with its own MCB (miniature circuit breaker), typically 20A or 32A depending on the unit\'s current draw. Older fuse boards without spare ways or without RCD protection may need replacing to accommodate the new circuit. A qualified electrician should assess this as part of the installation survey.</p><p><strong>Cable sizing and distance.</strong> The electrical cable run from the consumer unit to the heat pump must be correctly sized for the current draw and the distance. Longer runs require thicker cables to avoid excessive voltage drop. For a typical residential ASHP within 15–20 metres of the consumer unit, 6mm² or 10mm² twin-and-earth cable is usually sufficient. Your installer\'s electrician will calculate this based on the specific unit\'s requirements.</p><p><strong>Smart meter and tariff implications.</strong> A heat pump significantly increases your electricity consumption — typically by 3,000–5,000 kWh per year for a residential property. Many energy suppliers offer specific heat pump tariffs with lower unit rates, particularly for off-peak consumption. A smart meter is recommended (and increasingly required for new tariffs) to enable time-of-use pricing. Some heat pump controllers can be configured to run preferentially during off-peak periods, charging a thermal store with cheaper electricity.</p><p>When browsing this database, the <strong>peak electrical input (kW)</strong> figure tells you the maximum power draw under full load. This is the figure you need for fuse board and supply assessment. The actual draw will usually be lower than this, particularly in mild weather when the unit is modulating down.</p>'},
    {id:'f8',title:'What is the Boiler Upgrade Scheme?',short:'The Boiler Upgrade Scheme (BUS) is a UK government grant giving £7,500 off an air or ground source heat pump in England and Wales (£2,500 for air-to-air, £5,000 for biomass), applied upfront by your MCS-certified installer. An EPC is no longer required as of April 2026.',body:'<p>The <strong>Boiler Upgrade Scheme (BUS)</strong> is a UK government grant programme that provides financial support toward the installation of a heat pump or biomass boiler in homes and small non-domestic buildings in England and Wales. It is the primary financial incentive for heat pump adoption in the UK, funded through to 31 March 2030.</p><p><strong>Grant amounts.</strong> £7,500 toward an air source heat pump (ASHP) or ground source heat pump (GSHP, including water source and shared ground loops), £5,000 toward a biomass boiler (off-gas-grid, rural properties only), or £2,500 toward an air-to-air heat pump. Between 21 July 2026 and 31 March 2027, oil or LPG properties with no mains gas connection can get an extra £1,500 on top of the ASHP/GSHP grant — £9,000 in total. This is applied as an upfront discount — you do not pay the full cost and claim back later. The installer applies on your behalf, and the grant is deducted from your invoice.</p><p><strong>Eligibility criteria.</strong> You must own the property (including a business, second home, or rental) and be replacing a fossil fuel heating system (gas, oil, LPG, or electric). The property must be in England or Wales (Scotland and Northern Ireland have separate schemes). Your installer must commission and install the system within 120 days of applying for the grant.</p><p><strong>MCS certification requirement.</strong> The heat pump must be installed by an <strong>MCS-certified installer</strong> using an <strong>MCS-certified product</strong> — this is now a statutory requirement. Check that any product you\'re considering on this database is MCS-certified before assuming you can claim the grant against it.</p><p><strong>No EPC required.</strong> As of 28 April 2026, a valid Energy Performance Certificate is no longer needed to apply, and the old requirement to resolve outstanding loft/cavity-wall insulation recommendations first has been scrapped. Where a property has no EPC, the installer instead submits a recent utility bill and photos of the existing heating system.</p><p><strong>Application process.</strong> Get quotes from MCS-certified installers, confirm eligibility, and agree a quote. Your installer then applies for the grant through Ofgem\'s online system on your behalf. The grant amount is shown as a deduction on your quotation and invoice.</p><p><strong>What the grant covers.</strong> The grant is a contribution toward the total installed cost, not the whole bill. A typical residential ASHP installation costs £8,000–£16,000 depending on size and complexity, so the out-of-pocket cost after the grant is often £1,000–£8,500. GSHP installations are more expensive (£18,000–£35,000), so the grant covers a smaller proportion.</p><p><strong>Other financial support.</strong> Heat pumps also benefit from the reduced 0% VAT rate on energy-saving materials installed in residential properties. Some local authorities and lenders offer additional grants or preferential green mortgage rates.</p><p>Read our <a href="/knowledge/funding/" onclick="showPage(\'funding\');return false" style="color:#0D7377;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">full funding &amp; grants guide</a> for the complete requirements, payment terms, application steps and other UK schemes, or the official <a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-boiler-upgrade-scheme" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#0D7377;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">GOV.UK guidance</a>.</p>'},
    {id:'f9',title:'Can a heat pump provide cooling as well as heating?',short:'Yes — reversible heat pumps can provide active cooling in summer by running the refrigerant cycle in reverse, though not all models offer it. Cooling is best delivered through fan coil units or underfloor cooling. Check the \'mode\' field: heating-only units cannot cool, while heating & cooling units can do both.',body:'<p>Yes — many heat pumps are <strong>reversible</strong>, meaning they can switch direction and provide active cooling in summer as well as heating in winter. However, not all heat pumps offer this feature, and there are practical considerations around how cooling is delivered.</p><p><strong>How it works.</strong> A heat pump in heating mode absorbs heat from the environment (air, ground, or water) and transfers it into the building. In cooling mode, the refrigerant cycle reverses: the heat pump absorbs heat from inside the building and rejects it to the outside, functioning exactly like an air conditioning system. The same outdoor unit, the same compressor, and the same refrigerant — just running in the opposite direction.</p><p><strong>Which units offer cooling?</strong> In this database, every product is tagged as either "Heating only" or "Heating &amp; cooling" so you can filter accordingly. Generally, most units from Daikin, Samsung, Panasonic, Hitachi, LG, Toshiba, Midea, Ariston, and Fujitsu are reversible. Most units from Vaillant, Grant, Ideal Heating, Baxi, and Stiebel Eltron are heating only. This is not a quality distinction — heating-only units are often optimised for maximum heating efficiency, while reversible units include the additional valve and controls needed for cooling.</p><p><strong>How is cooling delivered?</strong> The cooling distribution method depends on your heating system. <strong>Underfloor heating (UFH)</strong> can double as underfloor cooling — chilled water circulates through the same pipes, cooling the floor surface. This provides gentle, draught-free cooling and is the most common method in European homes with heat pumps. However, the floor surface temperature must be controlled carefully (typically not below 18°C) to prevent condensation on the floor.</p><p><strong>Fan coil units</strong> provide more powerful active cooling, similar to conventional air conditioning. They circulate chilled water through a coil with a fan, actively blowing cool air into the room. They can cool a space faster than UFH but are more noticeable (visible units, fan noise). Radiators are generally not suitable for cooling — chilling water through radiators causes condensation to form on the cold surfaces.</p><p><strong>Passive cooling vs active cooling.</strong> Some ground source heat pumps offer <strong>passive cooling</strong> (also called free cooling or natural cooling) where cool water from the ground loop is circulated directly through the building\'s underfloor heating without running the compressor. This uses minimal electricity (just the circulation pumps) and provides gentle cooling. It is less powerful than active cooling but virtually free to run. Active cooling runs the compressor in reverse and provides stronger cooling effect but uses more electricity.</p><p><strong>Is cooling worth it in the UK?</strong> The UK has traditionally not needed air conditioning, but summers are getting warmer and the number of overheating hours in UK homes is increasing. Building Regulations Part O (2022) now requires overheating mitigation in new homes. A reversible heat pump provides cooling capability at relatively little additional cost compared to installing a separate air conditioning system. If you are installing underfloor heating anyway, the incremental cost of a reversible heat pump (versus heating-only) is typically modest.</p><p><strong>EER rating.</strong> When a heat pump operates in cooling mode, its efficiency is measured as the <strong>EER</strong> (Energy Efficiency Ratio) rather than COP. EER is calculated the same way — cooling output divided by electrical input. Typical EER values for residential heat pumps are 2.5–4.0. Some products in this database include their EER rating where the manufacturer provides it.</p>'},
    {id:'f10',title:'What does the F-Gas regulation mean for heat pumps?',short:'F-Gas regulations phase down high-GWP synthetic refrigerants (HFCs) over time, pushing the industry toward low-GWP options like R290 and R32. For buyers this means future-proofing matters: units using very low-GWP refrigerants are less likely to face rising servicing costs or supply restrictions as the phase-down tightens.',body:'<p>The <strong>F-Gas regulations</strong> control the use of fluorinated greenhouse gases (F-gases) — synthetic gases with high global warming potential that are widely used as refrigerants in heat pumps, air conditioning, and refrigeration. The UK regulations (retained from the EU F-Gas Regulation after Brexit) impose a gradual phase-down of HFC (hydrofluorocarbon) supply, with significant implications for heat pump technology and refrigerant choice.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 300" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="300" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="25" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">Global Warming Potential (GWP) Comparison</text>  <text x="280" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Lower GWP = less environmental impact. Values show CO₂-equivalent per kg of refrigerant.</text>  <!-- Y axis -->  <line x1="90" y1="60" x2="90" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1"/>  <text x="40" y="155" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10" font-weight="500" transform="rotate(-90,40,155)">GWP Value</text>  <!-- Y gridlines and labels -->  <line x1="90" y1="240" x2="510" y2="240" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5"/>  <text x="82" y="244" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">0</text>  <line x1="90" y1="197" x2="510" y2="197" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="201" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">500</text>  <line x1="90" y1="154" x2="510" y2="154" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="158" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">1,000</text>  <line x1="90" y1="111" x2="510" y2="111" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="115" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">1,500</text>  <line x1="90" y1="68" x2="510" y2="68" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>  <text x="82" y="72" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">2,000</text>  <!-- Bars (growing upward from baseline at y=240, scale: 2088 max maps to y=65) -->  <!-- R290: GWP 3 -->  <rect x="110" y="239.5" width="40" height="0.5" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="130" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">3</text>  <text x="130" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R290</text>  <text x="130" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">Propane</text>  <!-- R32: GWP 675 -->  <rect x="180" y="184" width="40" height="56" rx="2" fill="#3ECCC0"/>  <text x="200" y="178" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">675</text>  <text x="200" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R32</text>  <text x="200" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC</text>  <!-- R134a: GWP 1430 -->  <rect x="250" y="121" width="40" height="119" rx="2" fill="#C07B1A"/>  <text x="270" y="115" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C07B1A" font-size="12" font-weight="700">1,430</text>  <text x="270" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R134a</text>  <text x="270" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC</text>  <!-- R410A: GWP 2088 -->  <rect x="320" y="67" width="40" height="173" rx="2" fill="#B03030"/>  <text x="340" y="61" text-anchor="middle" fill="#B03030" font-size="12" font-weight="700">2,088</text>  <text x="340" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R410A</text>  <text x="340" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">HFC blend</text>  <!-- R744: GWP 1 -->  <rect x="390" y="239.9" width="40" height="0.1" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="410" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">1</text>  <text x="410" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R744</text>  <text x="410" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">CO₂</text>  <!-- R717: GWP 0 -->  <rect x="460" y="240" width="40" height="0" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="480" y="233" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="12" font-weight="700">0</text>  <text x="480" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">R717</text>  <text x="480" y="269" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="8">Ammonia</text>  <!-- Legend -->  <rect x="110" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#0D7377"/>  <text x="124" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">Natural / ultra-low GWP</text>  <rect x="280" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#3ECCC0"/>  <text x="294" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">Lower HFC</text>  <rect x="380" y="282" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#B03030"/>  <text x="394" y="291" fill="#3D5C5C" font-size="9">High GWP (phasing down)</text></svg></div><p><strong>What is being phased down?</strong> The regulations target HFCs based on their <strong>CO₂-equivalent</strong> impact. Each refrigerant has a Global Warming Potential (GWP) number — R410A has a GWP of 2088 (meaning 1 kg of R410A has the same warming effect as 2,088 kg of CO₂), R32 has a GWP of 675, while natural refrigerants like R290 (propane) have a GWP of just 3 and are essentially unaffected.</p><p>The phase-down works by progressively reducing the total quota of CO₂-equivalent HFCs that can be placed on the UK market each year. The target is to reduce HFC supply to 20% of baseline levels by 2030 and further reductions beyond that. This does not ban specific refrigerants outright — it reduces the total supply, which increases cost and encourages the market to shift toward lower-GWP alternatives.</p><p><strong>Which heat pump refrigerants are affected?</strong></p><p><strong>R410A</strong> (GWP 2088) is the most affected. Widely used in older ASHPs and still in some GSHP ranges, its high GWP means it consumes a large proportion of the available quota. Most manufacturers have already moved away from R410A for new products, but existing installations will continue to need R410A for servicing. Recycled and reclaimed R410A will remain available, but costs may increase.</p><p><strong>R32</strong> (GWP 675) is less immediately affected but still subject to quota constraints. Its lower GWP means it consumes roughly one-third of the quota that R410A does for the same charge weight. R32 will remain available for the foreseeable future, but the long-term regulatory direction is clearly toward even lower GWP options.</p><p><strong>R134a</strong> (GWP 1430) is used in some commercial and industrial water source heat pumps. Like R410A, its relatively high GWP means it faces significant phase-down pressure. Newer commercial units are moving to R513A (a lower-GWP HFO blend) or R290.</p><p><strong>Natural refrigerants</strong> — R290 (propane, GWP 3), R744 (CO₂, GWP 1), and R717 (ammonia, GWP 0) — are completely unaffected by F-Gas regulations. This is the primary regulatory driver behind the industry shift toward R290 in residential heat pumps and R744 in commercial and industrial applications.</p><p><strong>What this means for you.</strong> If you are buying a heat pump today, the F-Gas regulations should inform your refrigerant choice but not cause alarm. R32 units will be serviceable for their entire lifetime. R290 and R744 units offer complete regulatory certainty. If you have an existing R410A heat pump, it will continue to work — servicing with recycled refrigerant will remain possible, though costs may increase over time. The main practical impact is that the industry is innovating toward natural refrigerants, which is driving better products with higher efficiency and lower environmental impact.</p>'},
    {id:'f11',title:'How do operating temperatures affect heat pump performance?',short:'Temperature lift — the gap between the source and the flow temperature — is the biggest driver of efficiency. The smaller the lift, the higher the COP. Warmer sources (ground/water vs cold air) and lower flow temperatures (underfloor heating at 35°C vs radiators at 55°C) both raise efficiency. Designing for low flow temperatures is key.',body:'<p>Temperature is the single biggest factor affecting heat pump efficiency. Both the <strong>source temperature</strong> (where heat comes from) and the <strong>sink temperature</strong> (where heat goes to) directly determine the COP. Understanding this relationship is key to getting the most from a heat pump installation.</p><div style="margin:1.25rem 0;background:#f5f7f7;border:1px solid #d9e2e2;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;overflow-x:auto"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 260" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">  <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="260" fill="#f5f7f7" rx="8"/>  <text x="280" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0F2B2B" font-size="13" font-weight="600">How Flow Temperature Affects COP</text>  <!-- Axes -->  <line x1="80" y1="220" x2="520" y2="220" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <line x1="80" y1="50" x2="80" y2="220" stroke="#BDC9C9" stroke-width="1.5"/>  <text x="300" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10">Flow Temperature (°C)</text>  <text x="35" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="10" transform="rotate(-90,35,140)">COP</text>  <!-- X labels -->  <text x="140" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">35°C</text>  <text x="250" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">45°C</text>  <text x="360" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">55°C</text>  <text x="470" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">65°C</text>  <!-- Y labels -->  <text x="70" y="220" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">2</text>  <text x="70" y="163" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">3</text>  <text x="70" y="106" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">4</text>  <text x="70" y="54" text-anchor="end" fill="#7A9494" font-size="9">5</text>  <!-- COP curve -->  <path d="M140 63 C180 80 250 115 360 155 C420 175 450 185 470 195" fill="none" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="2.5"/>  <!-- UFH zone -->  <rect x="120" y="45" width="80" height="180" rx="0" fill="#0D7377" opacity=".08"/>  <text x="160" y="228" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0D7377" font-size="8" font-weight="500" letter-spacing=".05em">UFH ZONE</text>  <!-- Radiator zone -->  <rect x="320" y="45" width="120" height="180" rx="0" fill="#A66D1E" opacity=".08"/>  <text x="380" y="228" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="8" font-weight="500" letter-spacing=".05em">RADIATOR ZONE</text>  <!-- Annotations -->  <circle cx="140" cy="63" r="4" fill="#0D7377"/>  <line x1="144" y1="60" x2="195" y2="48" stroke="#0D7377" stroke-width="1" opacity=".4"/>  <text x="198" y="52" fill="#0D7377" font-size="10" font-weight="600">COP 4.8 at 35°C</text>  <circle cx="470" cy="195" r="4" fill="#A66D1E"/>  <text x="465" y="210" text-anchor="end" fill="#A66D1E" font-size="10" font-weight="600">COP 2.5 at 65°C</text></svg></div><p><strong>The fundamental principle.</strong> A heat pump works by moving heat from a lower temperature to a higher temperature. The bigger the temperature difference (or "lift") between source and sink, the harder the compressor has to work, and the lower the COP. This is a physical law (the Carnot cycle), not a limitation of any particular manufacturer or technology. Every heat pump in existence follows this principle.</p><p>In practical terms: an ASHP producing 35°C water on a 7°C day (lift of 28°C) will achieve a much higher COP than the same unit producing 65°C water on a −5°C day (lift of 70°C). The first scenario might give COP 4.5; the second might give COP 2.0. Same unit, same refrigerant — just different operating conditions.</p><p><strong>Flow temperature and heating system design.</strong> The flow temperature is the temperature of the water leaving the heat pump and entering your heating system. This is the most controllable factor in the equation and has the biggest impact on running costs.</p><p><strong>Underfloor heating (UFH)</strong> typically operates at 30–40°C flow temperature, which is ideal for heat pumps. The large surface area of the floor means lower water temperatures can deliver sufficient heat. With UFH, COPs of 4.0–5.0 are routinely achievable.</p><p><strong>Oversized radiators</strong> can operate at 40–50°C. If your existing radiators are larger than the minimum required for the room (which is common in older properties where radiators were specified with generous margins), a heat pump can often drive them at reduced flow temperatures. This may require a professional assessment — your installer should calculate whether each radiator can deliver the required heat output at the proposed flow temperature.</p><p><strong>Standard radiators</strong> designed for traditional boilers typically need 60–70°C flow temperatures. At these temperatures, heat pump COP drops significantly — typically to 2.5–3.0. High-temperature heat pumps (like the Samsung EHS HT range at 80°C or the Daikin Altherma 3 HT) can deliver these temperatures, but efficiency will be lower than with a low-temperature distribution system.</p><p><strong>Minimum ambient operating temperature.</strong> For ASHPs, the minimum ambient operating temperature tells you the coldest outdoor conditions at which the unit will continue to function. Modern units typically operate down to −20°C or −25°C, with some (Samsung, Midea) rated to −30°C. Below the rated minimum, the unit will shut down for safety. In the UK, outdoor temperatures very rarely fall below −15°C even in Scotland, so most modern ASHPs will operate year-round without issue.</p><p>However, COP drops significantly at very low ambient temperatures. A unit rated COP 4.5 at 7°C might only achieve COP 2.0 at −10°C. This is normal and expected — the seasonal COP (SCOP) accounts for this by averaging across all temperatures experienced during the heating season.</p><p><strong>Weather compensation.</strong> The best heat pump installations use <strong>weather compensation</strong> — automatically adjusting the flow temperature based on outdoor temperature. On mild days (10°C outside), the system might run at 30°C flow, achieving COP 5.0. On cold days (−5°C outside), it increases to 45°C, accepting a lower COP of 3.5 but maintaining comfort. This optimisation happens automatically and is the most effective way to maximise seasonal efficiency. Almost all modern heat pump controllers support weather compensation, and MCS installations are required to use it.</p>'},
    {id:'f12',title:'What is a transcritical CO₂ heat pump?',short:'A transcritical CO₂ (R744) heat pump operates above CO₂\'s critical point, enabling very high output temperatures — up to 90°C and beyond — that conventional refrigerants struggle to reach efficiently. With a GWP of 1, it excels at hot water and applications with a large temperature rise, mostly in commercial, industrial, and district heating settings.',body:'<p>A <strong>transcritical CO₂ heat pump</strong> uses carbon dioxide (R744) as its refrigerant and operates across the critical point of CO₂ — a unique thermodynamic regime that enables very high output temperatures that conventional refrigerants cannot efficiently achieve. These are specialist machines, primarily used in commercial, industrial, and district heating applications.</p><p><strong>Why CO₂ is different.</strong> Most refrigerants used in heat pumps (R32, R290, R410A) operate in a subcritical cycle — the refrigerant condenses from gas to liquid in the condenser, releasing heat at a relatively constant temperature. CO₂ has an unusually low critical point (31.1°C at 73.8 bar), which means that in a typical heating application, the high-pressure side of the cycle operates above the critical point — hence "transcritical." Above the critical point, there is no distinct phase change; instead, the CO₂ undergoes a continuous temperature glide as it cools, releasing heat over a wide temperature range.</p><p><strong>The practical advantage.</strong> This temperature glide is actually a benefit for heating water. In a conventional heat pump, the condenser releases heat at a nearly constant temperature — efficient for space heating but wasteful for heating water from, say, 10°C to 65°C, because the refrigerant temperature must be above 65°C for the entire process. In a transcritical CO₂ cycle, the gas cooler (equivalent of the condenser) releases heat over a gliding temperature range — perhaps from 90°C down to 25°C. This matches beautifully with the counter-flow heating of water from cold to hot, resulting in very high efficiency for domestic hot water production.</p><p>This is why CO₂ heat pumps excel at producing high-temperature hot water (70–90°C) directly, without the efficiency penalty that conventional refrigerants suffer at these temperatures. A CO₂ heat pump can deliver 90°C water with a COP of 3.0–3.5, whereas an R32 unit producing the same temperature might only achieve COP 1.8–2.2.</p><p><strong>Applications in the UK.</strong> Transcritical CO₂ heat pumps are used in several key applications in the UK market. <strong>District heating</strong> schemes use large CO₂ heat pumps (500 kW to several MW) to supply high-temperature water to heat networks. The Fenagy H-range and Star Renewable Energy Neatpump in this database are examples — they supply 85–90°C water for district heating from river, sea, or wastewater sources.</p><p><strong>Commercial hot water</strong> applications (hotels, hospitals, leisure centres) benefit from CO₂ heat pumps\' ability to efficiently heat large volumes of water to 65°C+ for legionella compliance. The Mitsubishi Heavy Industries QAHV and Clade Maple ranges serve this market, delivering 70°C+ water from an air-source configuration.</p><p><strong>Industrial process heat</strong> at temperatures up to 120–170°C is possible with specialised CO₂ and hydrocarbon cascade systems, such as those offered by Solid Energy. These replace gas-fired boilers in food processing, chemical manufacturing, and other industrial applications.</p><p><strong>Why not use CO₂ for residential?</strong> Transcritical CO₂ systems operate at very high pressures (80–130 bar on the high side, compared to 10–30 bar for R32/R290). This requires specialist components, heavier construction, and more complex controls. The cost and complexity are justified for large commercial installations but are currently too high for typical residential applications. Some manufacturers are developing smaller CO₂ units for residential use, but R290 and R32 remain the dominant residential choices for now.</p><p><strong>Environmental credentials.</strong> CO₂ has a GWP of 1 — the lowest possible for any greenhouse gas by definition. It is non-flammable, non-toxic, and globally abundant. It is completely unaffected by F-Gas regulations. For operators seeking the lowest possible direct environmental impact with the ability to deliver very high temperatures, transcritical CO₂ is the technology of choice.</p>'},
    {id:'f13',title:'Glossary of Heat Pump Terms',body:'<p>A quick-reference guide to the technical terms used throughout this database and in heat pump product specifications.</p><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Heat Pump Types</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">ASHP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Air Source Heat Pump. Extracts heat from outdoor air using a refrigerant cycle. The most common type of heat pump installed in UK homes.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">GSHP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Ground Source Heat Pump. Extracts heat from the ground via buried pipes (horizontal trenches or vertical boreholes). Higher COP than ASHPs but more expensive to install.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">WSHP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Water Source Heat Pump. Extracts heat from a body of water such as a river, lake, or borehole. Offers the highest COPs but requires access to a suitable water source and Environment Agency permits.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Monobloc</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A heat pump design where the compressor, heat exchanger, and all refrigerant components are contained in a single outdoor unit. Only water pipes connect to the building — no refrigerant lines run indoors. Simpler installation and lower F-Gas risk.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Split System</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A heat pump design with an outdoor unit (containing the evaporator and compressor) connected to an indoor unit (containing the condenser) by refrigerant pipes. Allows greater capacity but requires F-Gas qualified installation.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Performance Metrics</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">COP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Coefficient of Performance. The ratio of heat output to electrical input at a specific test point. A COP of 4.0 means the unit produces 4 kW of heat for every 1 kW of electricity consumed.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">SCOP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Seasonal Coefficient of Performance. The average COP across an entire heating season, weighted by how many hours are spent at each outdoor temperature. A more realistic measure of real-world efficiency than COP.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">EER</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Energy Efficiency Ratio. The cooling equivalent of COP — measures cooling output divided by electrical input when the heat pump operates in reverse (cooling mode).</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">SEER</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio. The seasonal average of EER across a cooling season, analogous to SCOP for heating.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Carnot Cycle</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The theoretical maximum efficiency of any heat pump, determined by the temperature difference between the heat source and heat sink. Real heat pumps achieve 40–60% of Carnot efficiency. Smaller temperature lifts give higher efficiency.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Bivalent Point</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The outdoor temperature below which a heat pump can no longer meet the full heating demand alone and a supplementary heat source (electric heater or boiler) kicks in. Good system design aims to keep this below −5°C for the UK.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Refrigerants</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Refrigerant</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The working fluid inside a heat pump that absorbs and releases heat as it changes between liquid and gas states. Common types include R290 (propane), R32, R410A, R744 (CO₂), and R600a (isobutane).</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R290</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Propane. A natural hydrocarbon refrigerant with a GWP of 3. Increasingly used in residential ASHPs due to excellent thermodynamic properties and minimal environmental impact. Classified as A3 (flammable).</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R32</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Difluoromethane. A synthetic HFC refrigerant with a GWP of 675. Widely used in residential and commercial heat pumps. Lower GWP than R410A but still subject to F-Gas phase-down regulations.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R410A</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">An HFC refrigerant blend with a GWP of 2088. Previously the dominant heat pump refrigerant, now being phased out in favour of lower-GWP alternatives like R32 and R290.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R744</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Carbon dioxide (CO₂). A natural refrigerant with a GWP of 1. Used in transcritical heat pumps for commercial and industrial applications where high output temperatures (70–90°C) are required.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R600a</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Isobutane. A natural hydrocarbon refrigerant with a GWP of 3. Used in water-source industrial heat pumps, capable of supply temperatures up to 95°C.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">R717</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Ammonia. A natural refrigerant with a GWP of 0. Used in large industrial and district heating heat pumps. Toxic and mildly flammable, so restricted to specialist installations.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">GWP</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Global Warming Potential. A measure of how much heat a greenhouse gas traps relative to CO₂ over 100 years. Lower GWP is better. R290 has a GWP of 3; R410A has a GWP of 2,088.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Transcritical</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A refrigeration cycle operating above the critical point of the refrigerant. Applies to CO₂ (R744) heat pumps where the high-pressure side exceeds 73.8 bar and 31.1°C. Enables very high output temperatures with good efficiency.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Noise & Acoustics</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">LwA</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Sound Power Level (A-weighted), measured in dB(A). The total acoustic energy output of the heat pump, independent of distance. This is the figure most manufacturers quote in datasheets.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">LpA</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Sound Pressure Level (A-weighted), measured in dB(A). What you actually hear at a specific distance from the unit. Decreases by approximately 6 dB each time you double the distance. UK planning limit is 42 dB(A) LpA at the nearest neighbour window.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">System Components & Controls</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Inverter</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A variable-speed compressor drive that allows the heat pump to modulate its output up and down to match demand, rather than switching on and off at full capacity. Improves efficiency, reduces cycling, and lowers noise.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Buffer Tank</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A water storage vessel (typically 50–200 litres) installed between the heat pump and the heating system. Provides thermal mass to reduce compressor cycling, smooth load variations, and allow the heat pump to run at optimal efficiency for longer periods.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">DHW</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Domestic Hot Water. The hot water used for taps, showers, and baths, as opposed to space heating. Heat pumps typically heat a DHW cylinder to 45–55°C, with periodic legionella pasteurisation cycles to 60°C+.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Defrost Cycle</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">When an ASHP operates in cold, humid conditions, frost builds up on the outdoor heat exchanger. The defrost cycle briefly reverses the refrigerant flow to melt the ice. Modern units defrost intelligently based on sensor readings rather than fixed timers.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Weather Compensation</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">An automatic control strategy that adjusts the heat pump flow temperature based on outdoor temperature. Warmer days get lower flow temperatures (higher COP); colder days get higher flow temperatures. Essential for maximising seasonal efficiency.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Temperatures & Operating Conditions</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Flow Temperature</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The temperature of the water leaving the heat pump and entering the heating system. Lower flow temperatures give higher COP. Underfloor heating typically uses 30–40°C; radiators typically need 45–65°C.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Return Temperature</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The temperature of the water returning from the heating system to the heat pump. The difference between flow and return is the temperature differential (ΔT), typically 5–10°C for radiators.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">A7/W35</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">A common COP test condition: 7°C outdoor air temperature, 35°C water flow temperature. This is a favourable test point. Real-world conditions vary, which is why SCOP is a more useful measure.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">Legionella</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Legionella bacteria can grow in water stored between 20–45°C. DHW cylinders must periodically reach 60°C to kill bacteria. Heat pumps achieve this via an immersion heater boost or by running at elevated temperatures during pasteurisation cycles.</span></div></div><h4 style="margin-top:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:#0F2B2B;letter-spacing:-0.01em;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid #0D7377">Standards, Schemes & Regulations</h4><div style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">MCS</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Microgeneration Certification Scheme. The UK quality assurance framework for renewable energy installations. Both the heat pump product and the installer must be MCS-certified for the installation to qualify for the Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">BUS</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Boiler Upgrade Scheme. A UK government grant providing £7,500 toward the cost of an ASHP or GSHP installation in England and Wales. Requires MCS-certified installer and product.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">F-Gas</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">Fluorinated greenhouse gases — synthetic refrigerants (HFCs like R32, R410A, R134a) regulated under UK and EU F-Gas legislation. Supply is being progressively phased down based on CO₂-equivalent quotas.</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">EN 14511</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The European standard for testing heat pump performance at specific operating conditions. Defines how COP is measured at stated air/water temperatures (e.g. A7/W35 means 7°C air, 35°C water flow).</span></div><div style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e2"><strong style="color:#0D7377;font-family:DM Mono,monospace;font-size:13px">EN 14825</strong><br><span style="font-size:13px;color:#3D5C5C;line-height:1.7">The European standard for calculating SCOP. Defines the temperature bins and weighting factors used to calculate seasonal performance across a heating season for different European climate zones.</span></div></div>'}
  ]
};
